<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tomás Mejía</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1820">1820</a>–<a href="/years/1867">1867</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tomás Mejía was a Mexican general of Otomí origin from the Sierra Gorda of Querétaro and a leading conservative commander in the Reform War and the French intervention. He fought for Emperor Maximilian to the end and was captured with him at Querétaro. He was executed with Maximilian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-miramon">Miguel Miramón</a> on 19 June 1867.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tomas-mejia.jpg" alt="Tomás Mejía" width="320" height="510" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Louis de Planque (1842-1898) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomas_mejia_c.1865.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>José Tomás de la Luz Mejía Camacho was born on 17 September 1820, probably in Pinal de Amoles, and went to school in Jalpan, where his father Cristóbal Mejía was prefect from 1840 to 1842. The rebel general José de Urrea, sheltered in the family's house, told him war stories, and when government troops under Juan Cano arrived he enlisted. Cano recommended him to President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anastasio-bustamante">Anastasio Bustamante</a> for the Military Academy. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apache">Apache</a> from Chihuahua from 1842 to 1845 and saw action at the Battle of Buena Vista in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican-American War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1850 he was military commander of the Sierra Gorda. After the Lerdo law of 1856 stripped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> and Indigenous communities of their communal lands, he seized Querétaro in October, promising to defend the church and the communities' lands, and then waged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war from the Sierra. In the Reform War he joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-miramon">Miguel Miramón</a> to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-luis-potosi">San Luis Potosí</a> in September 1858. After the liberals took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> in January 1861 he fought on, capturing Río Verde and its commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariano-escobedo">Mariano Escobedo</a>, whom he released. President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-juarez">Benito Juárez</a> put a price of 10,000 pesos on his head.</p><p>Mejía at first waited on the French intervention, then backed the Second Mexican Empire with Maximilian of Habsburg as its monarch. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-luis-potosi">San Luis Potosí</a> on 25 December 1863 and in 1864 defeated Manuel Doblado at Matehuala, for which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> gave him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> of Honour. As American supplies reached the republicans after 1865, he withdrew from Matamoros in June 1866 and joined Maximilian at Querétaro.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Escobedo's republican army appeared before Querétaro on 12 March 1867, and Mejía helped to repulse its assaults until the imperial leaders were captured on 15 May. Escobedo offered him a way to escape, but Mejía refused it unless Maximilian and Miramón could go with him. A court martial presided over by Rafael Platón Sánchez heard that he had spared Escobedo and Jerónimo Treviño, yet sentenced him to death.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tomás Mejía” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Napoleon III recalled his troops in 1866–1867, and republican armies besieged Maximilian at Querétaro, where he surrendered in May 1867 and was shot on 19 June together with the conservative generals Miguel Miramón and <b>Tomás Mejía</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mexico">Mexico · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Maximilian stayed, was besieged at Querétaro and captured, and was shot with generals Miramón and <b>Mejía</b> on 19 June 1867.</blockquote><a href="/state/second-mexican-empire">Second Mexican Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Pinal de Amoles Municipality, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-queretaro">Santiago de Querétaro</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.16,-99.55&amp;year=1850" aria-label="Pinal de Amoles Municipality on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Tomás Mejía"><image href="/maps/globes/g270n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4242 5721h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4242 5721h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.16,-99.55&amp;year=1850">What was here in 1850? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
